  "updated": "2026-09-03T16:28:32.622716+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nFor this morning... SPC mesoanalysis continues to showcase MUCAPE \nvalues around 2000 J/kg along with 35-40 knots of effective bulk \nshear with sufficient mid-level lapse rates around 5.5-6C/km. These \nconditions have supported repeated rounds of thunderstorms, some \nstrong to severe, primarily along and and between M59 to I94. \nInitiation has taken place along a quasi-stationary frontal zone \nwhich has been reinforced from prior convective outflow. Additional \nshowers and storms remain possible through this morning as this \nboundary provides a mechanism for ascent within this subtropical \nairmass. Given the degree of elevated instability and bulk shear, \nisolated strong to severe thunderstorms cannot be ruled out through \nthe morning. \n\nWarm and humid conditions are again expected this afternoon \ndewpoints again holding in the upper 60s to low 70s. Current \nthinking is that temperatures will be slightly lower relative to \nyesterday which decreases probabilities to reach heat advisory \nthresholds. Additionally, given where the frontal boundary is, some \npotential exists for showers and thunderstorms through the late \nmorning and into the afternoon which if materialized, could temper \ntemperatures locally. Although several hi-res solutions continue to \nretain an early day period of clearing and stronger insolation. \nRegardless, temperatures will be capable of pushing back into the \nupper 80s to low 90s through the MI/OH border into the Metro region \nwith heat indices in the mid to upper 90s. \n\nFor this afternoon and evening, SE MI will be susceptible to renewed \nthunderstorm activity. One possible scenario will stream from \nactivity upstream across Wisconsin or the western Great Lakes as a \nstrengthening LLJ pools moisture and enhances convergence along the \nstalled frontal zone. Mean flow and forecasted storm motion would \ncarry this activity SE towards lower Michigan through the afternoon \nand evening, placing all of SE MI within the corridor for potential \nstrong to severe thunderstorm development. Surface based instability \nwill increase with southward extend. SBCAPE around 1500-2000 J/kg is \npossible near the MI/OH border and tapers towards 1000 J/kg north of \nI-69. Deep layer shear around 30-35 knots remains solid for \norganized convection with all storm modes possible. \n\nDamaging wind gusts will remain the primary hazard when taking into \naccount strong low-level lapse rates, high PW, and strong buoyancy. \nThis will support efficient precipitation loading for wet \nmicrobursts and/or consolidation of outflows with cold pool dynamics \nthat can support bowing segments. Isolate large hail also remains \npossible given improving mid-level lapse rates approaching 6.5C/km. \nA large portion of SE MI has been upgraded to a Slight Risk in the \nlatest SWODY 1, aligned where the better daytime instability is \nprojected to reside. \n\nAdditional rounds of showers and storms then becomes increasingly \nlikely late tonight into Friday morning as an elevated warm front \nstrengthens from SE WI into lower Michigan. This has been a pretty \nrobust signal in the latest 00Z runs. The boost in low to mid-level \ntheta a with convergence along this boundary will help support an \narea of showers and storms overnight. While surface base instability \ndiminishes during this time, elevated instability and strong shear \nholds which will maintain potential for organized convection. Storm \nthreat would transition to hail given the more elevated nature of \nthis activity along with localized heavy rainfall and flooding, \nespecially with any repeated convection along the boundary. Some \nuncertainty resides how far east this activity will extend, but the \ntrends still take activity to or south of M59 overnight.\n\nFriday will again offer additional chances for rain and thunderstorm \nbefore ridge amplification across the Plains aids in reinforcing \nhigh pressure over the Great Lakes, which will work to decrease rain \nand thunderstorm chances this weekend.",
